# Polynomial Interpolation Methods

A Python implementation and visualization of classical polynomial interpolation methods, developed as part of the **Mathematical Foundations for AI** coursework.

The project implements three interpolation techniques:

* Newton interpolation
* Lagrange interpolation
* Hermite interpolation

It also includes a visualization of the **Runge phenomenon**, which illustrates the oscillations that can appear when using high-degree polynomial interpolation with equally spaced points.

## 📌 Project Overview

Polynomial interpolation is a numerical method used to approximate a function from a finite set of known data points.

Given a set of points

$$
(x_0,y_0), (x_1,y_1), \ldots, (x_n,y_n)
$$

the objective is to construct a polynomial \(P(x)\) such that:

$$
P(x_i)=y_i
$$

for all interpolation points.

This project provides implementations of different interpolation approaches and visualizes their behavior using Python and Matplotlib.

## 🧮 Implemented Methods

### 1. Newton Interpolation

Newton interpolation constructs the interpolation polynomial using divided differences.

The polynomial has the form:

$$
P(x)=a_0+a_1(x-x_0)+a_2(x-x_0)(x-x_1)+\cdots
$$

The implementation computes the divided-difference table and evaluates the resulting polynomial at any given point.

### 2. Lagrange Interpolation

The Lagrange interpolation polynomial is defined by:

$$
P(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}y_iL_i(x)
$$

where:

$$
L_i(x)=\prod_{\substack{j=0\\j\neq i}}^n
\frac{x-x_j}{x_i-x_j}
$$

This method directly constructs the polynomial from the interpolation points.

### 3. Hermite Interpolation

Hermite interpolation extends polynomial interpolation by using both function values and derivative values.

For each interpolation point \(x_i\), the following information can be provided:

$$
f(x_i), \qquad f'(x_i)
$$

This allows the interpolation polynomial to match not only the function values but also the derivatives at the interpolation points.

## 📊 Visualizations

The project generates visualizations comparing:

* The original function
* Interpolation points
* Newton interpolation
* Lagrange interpolation
* Hermite interpolation

The project also demonstrates the **Runge phenomenon**.

### Runge Phenomenon

The Runge phenomenon occurs when a high-degree polynomial is used to interpolate a function at equally spaced points.

A classical example is the Runge function:

$$
f(x)=\frac{1}{1+25x^2}, \qquad x\in[-1,1]
$$

When the number of equally spaced interpolation points increases, significant oscillations can appear, particularly near the boundaries of the interval.

This phenomenon demonstrates an important limitation of high-degree polynomial interpolation.

